# React composition

UI API: **hierarchical filenames + compound statics + default slots**.

Full assembly (every file, root → leaf → compose edge): `react-compound-e2e.mdc`.

## Filename encodes the tree

```
file-explorer.tsx
file-explorer-toolbar.tsx
file-explorer-toolbar-title.tsx
file-explorer-list.tsx
file-explorer-row.tsx
file-explorer-row-name.tsx
```

One file ≈ one node. Attach children as statics:

```ts
FileExplorer.Toolbar = FileExplorerToolbar;
FileExplorerToolbar.Title = FileExplorerToolbarTitle;
FileExplorer.List = FileExplorerList;
FileExplorer.Row = FileExplorerRow;
FileExplorerRow.Name = FileExplorerRowName;
```

Call site reads like the tree: `FileExplorer.Row.Name`.

## Compose edge maps collections

The parent lays out chrome and provides context. The **caller** maps the list:

```tsx
import { FileExplorer } from '@repo/react/design-system';

import { useSpaceFiles } from '../hooks/use-space-files';

export function SpaceManage({ spaceId }: SpaceManageProps) {
  const { files, dispatch } = useSpaceFiles({ spaceId });

  return (
    <FileExplorer dispatch={dispatch}>
      <FileExplorer.Toolbar />
      {files.map((file) => (
        <FileExplorer.Row key={file.id} file={file} />
      ))}
    </FileExplorer>
  );
}
```

`FileExplorer` renders `{children}` (and default slots). It does not call `files.map` itself.

## Stable keys

Keys are domain identity, nested when the list is nested:

```tsx
<FileExplorer.Row key={file.id} file={file} />
<ScoreCell key={`${file.id}-score-${scoreIndex}`} />
```

## Default slots for variation

**Named slot:**

```tsx
import { Stack, type StackProps } from '../../stack/feature/stack';
import type { WithoutStyle } from '../../util/without-style';
import type { SpaceFile } from '../util/types';

export function FileExplorerList({
  files,
  empty = (
    <>
      <FileExplorerList.Empty>
        <FileExplorerList.Empty.Title />
        <FileExplorerList.Empty.Hint />
      </FileExplorerList.Empty>
    </>
  ),
  children,
  ...props
}: {
  files: SpaceFile[];
  empty?: React.ReactNode;
  children?: React.ReactNode;
} & WithoutStyle<Omit<StackProps, 'children'>>) {
  return (
    <FileExplorerListContext.Provider value={{ files }}>
      <Stack gap="tight" {...props}>
        {files.length === 0 ? empty : children}
      </Stack>
    </FileExplorerListContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Override by passing `empty={…}` or `empty={null}`.

**Default `children`:**

```tsx
children = (
  <>
    <FileExplorerRow.Thumb />
    <FileExplorerRow.Info>
      <FileExplorerRow.Name />
      <FileExplorerRow.Meta />
    </FileExplorerRow.Info>
    <FileExplorerRow.Actions />
  </>
)
```

For status-like UI, prefer one discriminant prop (`status: 'ready' | 'error' | …`) or separate thin wrappers alongside slots.

## Context per nesting level

Compound parents that many leaves need provide a small context + throwing accessor:

```ts
export function useFileExplorerDispatch() {
  const ctx = useContext(FileExplorerDispatchContext);
  if (!ctx) {
    throw new Error(
      '`useFileExplorerDispatch` must be used within a `FileExplorerDispatchContext`',
    );
  }
  return ctx;
}
```

Same pattern: `useFileExplorerListContext`, `useFileExplorerRowContext`.

Context value is always a small object (`value={{ file }}`, `value={{ dispatch }}`), even for one field — so leaves destructure a stable shape.

Context carries **ambient identity / dispatch** for the compound tree. The compose edge still passes `dispatch` / `files` / `file` into the parent that provides the context.

Leaves build the payload and dispatch:

```tsx
import { Input } from '@repo/react/design-system';
import { renameFile } from '../util/rename-file';
import { useFileExplorerDispatch } from '../feature/file-explorer';
import { useFileExplorerRowContext } from './file-explorer-row';

export function FileExplorerRowName() {
  const dispatch = useFileExplorerDispatch();
  const { file } = useFileExplorerRowContext();

  return (
    <Input
      defaultValue={file.name}
      onBlur={(event) =>
        dispatch(renameFile({ fileId: file.id, name: event.currentTarget.value }))
      }
    />
  );
}
```

## Props

- Layout wrappers extend `WithoutStyle<StackProps | GroupProps>` (`design-system-layout.mdc`).
- Content leaves use DS `Input` / `Pin` / `Text` props (`id`, `aria-*`, `value`, `onChange`).
- No public `className`, `classNames`, `style`, or `styles` on any public component.

```tsx
import { Text } from '../../text/feature/text';

export function FileExplorerToolbarTitle() {
  const { title } = useFileExplorerToolbarContext();
  return <Text variant="title">{title}</Text>;
}
```

## Event merging

When a leaf runs its logic and honors caller handlers, use `callAll`:

```ts
export function callAll<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(
  ...fns: (T | undefined)[]
) {
  return (...args: Parameters<T>) => {
    fns.forEach((fn) => void fn?.(...args));
  };
}

onBlur={callAll(onBlur, handleStopEditing)}
```

## Two modes, two returns

When a control has two visual trees (read vs edit, empty vs filled), return each tree in full — one `if` at the top of the function — rather than one markup soup of nested ternaries.

```tsx
import { Input, type InputProps, Text } from '@repo/react/design-system';

export type EditableTextProps = Omit<InputProps, 'autoFocus'>;

export function EditableText({ readOnly = false, defaultValue = '', ...props }: EditableTextProps) {
  const { value: isEditing, setTrue: handleStartEditing, setFalse: handleStopEditing } =
    useBoolean();

  if (isEditing) {
    return <Input autoFocus defaultValue={defaultValue} onBlur={handleStopEditing} {...props} />;
  }

  return (
    <Text variant="label">{defaultValue}</Text>
  );
}
```
